INT. HOSPITAL ROOM 412 - NIGHT

Monitors blink. A drip counts the seconds.

DR. YASMIN HADDAD, 40s, stands by the bed with a chart. NURSE FLORENCE OYELARAN, 50s, adjusts a pillow. In the bed lies MRS. CONSTANCE ABERCROMBIE, 80s, awake, small as a bird.

CONSTANCE
Is it bad?

YASMIN
We're still running tests.

FLORENCE
She means it's not good.

YASMIN
Florence.

FLORENCE
She asked.

CONSTANCE
I did ask.

YASMIN
Mrs. Abercrombie--

CONSTANCE
Call me Connie. Everyone does.

YASMIN
Connie, the scan shows a mass.

FLORENCE
(overlapping)
We'll know more tomorrow.

CONSTANCE
(overlapping)
How big?

YASMIN
Four centimetres.

CONSTANCE
That's the size of a plum.

FLORENCE
A small plum.

CONSTANCE
I like plums.

Yasmin lowers the chart. She sits on the edge of the bed. She takes the old woman's hand.

YASMIN
We'll do everything we can.

CONSTANCE
You're a kind girl.

YASMIN
I'm trying to be.

FLORENCE
(quietly)
She is.

CONSTANCE
Now. Would one of you please find me a plum?
